<div class="start-card"><p><strong>You will learn:</strong> schema migration versus data, vector shape, private networking, and the distinction between settings, secrets and identity.</p><p><strong>Starting requirements:</strong> stages 01–02 were submitted successfully; images have been built. Slow provisioning may still be running.</p><p><strong>Shell and directory:</strong> Bash, repository root. The scripts isolate kubectl in <code>workshop/.state/kubeconfig</code>; set that path explicitly for manual kubectl commands.</p></div>
<h2 id="ready">Wait, then configure access</h2>
{{snippet:commands.sh:configure}}
<p><code>03-ready.sh</code> waits for actual ARM completion, reports failed operations, gets this cluster's credentials into its own kubeconfig, waits for a Ready node and checks the PostgreSQL extension allowlist. It does not open a public PostgreSQL firewall rule. Your laptop normally cannot connect directly to the private database.</p>
<p><code>04-configure.sh</code> assigns data-plane roles, creates federated credentials, grants the kubelet identity <code>AcrPull</code>, configures KEDA's own identity, and writes ordinary settings. It retries boundedly for directory/RBAC propagation. Resource creation permissions do not automatically grant a workload permission to read a blob, secret, setting or queue.</p>
<h2 id="identity">Follow identity to settings and secrets</h2>
<figure class="flow" aria-label="Identity and configuration architecture"><div class="flow-node"><strong>AKS service account</strong>Annotated client ID + labeled Pod</div><span class="flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span><div class="flow-node"><strong>Federated identity</strong>AKS OIDC token exchanged for Entra token</div><span class="flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span><div class="flow-node"><strong>App Configuration</strong>Endpoints, deployments, counts</div><span class="flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span><div class="flow-node"><strong>Key Vault</strong>Retrieve actual database/browser passwords</div><figcaption>Functions uses its assigned managed identity directly. Every application's ordinary settings and secrets are read once at startup.</figcaption></figure>
<p>AKS workload identity requires all three: the service account's <code>azure.workload.identity/client-id</code> annotation, the Pod's <code>azure.workload.identity/use: "true"</code> label, and an Azure federated credential with the exact issuer, subject and audience. The webhook injects token-file/environment bootstrap. See {{file:k8s/products.yaml}} and {{file:scripts/04-configure.sh}}.</p>
<p>The only application-specific bootstrap environment settings are <code>APP_CONFIG_ENDPOINT</code> and its <code>workshop</code> label. The SDK credential uses the workload/managed identity. App Configuration supplies the Key Vault URI and ordinary service settings. Then the application reads the PostgreSQL password from Key Vault. The Java application also reads its browser sign-in password there. These values are not Kubernetes environment variables or image layers.</p>
<table><thead><tr><th>Mechanism</th><th>What this workshop puts there</th><th>What it does not do</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Key Vault</td><td>Actual PostgreSQL and Products sign-in passwords</td><td>Does not replace the application's database connection or encrypt its SQL automatically</td></tr><tr><td>App Configuration</td><td>Endpoints, model deployment names, retrieval count, chunk size</td><td>Is not used as a password store</td></tr><tr><td>Kubernetes ConfigMap</td><td>The non-secret App Configuration endpoint and label</td><td>Is not a secret manager</td></tr><tr><td>Kubernetes Secret</td><td>The demo endpoint's TLS certificate/private key</td><td>Is not Key Vault; access still depends on Kubernetes RBAC</td></tr><tr><td>Environment variables</td><td>Bootstrap selectors and SDK-injected identity metadata</td><td>Are a process input mechanism, not a secure secret service</td></tr><tr><td>Managed/workload identity</td><td>Short-lived tokens tied to the Function or service account</td><td>Does not grant permissions until Azure roles are assigned</td></tr></tbody></table>
<h2 id="schema">Read the Flyway migration</h2>
<p>Products runs the supplied Flyway migration during startup, before serving application requests. Flyway records its version/checksum in <code>flyway_schema_history</code>. A second startup validates/reuses the existing migration; do not edit an applied migration to change a live schema.</p>
<p>{{file:apps/products/src/main/resources/db/migration/V1__products_and_messages.sql}} creates <code>vector</code> and the product, chunk, chat, outbox and result-cache tables. PostgreSQL must first allowlist <code>VECTOR</code>; Bicep does that at server level. Flyway then runs as <code>workshopadmin</code>, the database owner created by this workshop. That account can create the allowlisted extension and all tables.</p>
<p>The core deliberately uses the same database owner's credentials in all three applications to keep the first deployment small. They are stored only in Key Vault and retrieved at startup. This grants broader database access than a production application should have. Independent migration/runtime SQL roles or Entra PostgreSQL authentication are optional extensions, not secretly assumed setup.</p>
<table><thead><tr><th>Invariant</th><th>Implementation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Document/question vector agreement</td><td>Both call the <code>embedding</code> deployment and request 1536 dimensions; the table is <code>vector(1536)</code>. Code rejects non-finite or incorrectly sized vectors.</td></tr><tr><td>Retry-safe chunks</td><td>Primary key <code>(manual_id, chunk_index)</code>; all chunk writes and the ready state commit atomically.</td></tr><tr><td>Simple retrieval</td><td>Exact cosine scan using <code>&lt;=&gt;</code>, optional product ID filter, configurable count and minimum similarity.</td></tr><tr><td>Persistent asynchronous chat</td><td>Questions + outbox commit together; worker results are cached by request ID; terminal replies cannot overwrite a displayed answer.</td></tr></tbody></table>
<h2 id="ordering">Notice upload ordering</h2>
<p>The product row commits <em>before</em> Blob upload starts. If upload fails, Products marks the record failed. If a process dies between those operations, the record may remain waiting; the timeout sweep marks old work failed. A participant retries by creating a new product/manual, not by mutating the old blob. The embedder checks the stored checksum and IDs before indexing.</p>
<details class="optional"><summary>Optional: inspect the full SQL or think about production changes</summary>{{code:apps/products/src/main/resources/db/migration/V1__products_and_messages.sql}}<p>The small dataset does not justify an approximate-nearest-neighbor index. For a large corpus, compare an exact scan with HNSW/IVFFlat, recall, memory and query plans. An index is a measured optimization, not a required RAG decoration.</p></details>
<h2 id="verify">Verification and common failures</h2>
<div class="verification"><strong>Verification:</strong> stage 03 should report a Ready node and <code>VECTOR</code>; stage 04 should finish configuration without printing secret values. Flyway runs in the next chapter when Products starts. No fabricated SQL output is presented here.</div>
<ul><li><strong>PostgreSQL hostname resolves publicly or not at all inside a Pod:</strong> inspect the private DNS zone's VNet link and the database's delegated subnet. Do not solve it with a public <code>0.0.0.0/0</code> firewall rule.</li><li><strong>Key Vault / App Configuration returns 403:</strong> check the workload's principal ID and the exact resource scope; wait for the bounded propagation retries. A CLI login as yourself does not configure the Pod identity.</li><li><strong>Flyway cannot create vector:</strong> check <code>azure.extensions</code> and confirm you are using the configured database owner. Do not use <code>pgvector</code> as the SQL extension name; the extension is <code>vector</code>.</li><li><strong>KEDA later shows authentication errors:</strong> the scaler has a separate identity/federated credential, not the worker's service account. Its configuration is completed here for the later exercise.</li></ul>
<p><strong>Continue when:</strong> the cluster, database, identities and ordinary settings are ready. Next, deploy Products and observe Flyway startup.</p>
